### What is the Valid Parentheses problem?

The problem asks whether a string containing only round `()`, square `[]`, and curly `{}` brackets is balanced and properly nested. Every opening bracket must have a matching closing bracket of the same type, and they must close in the reverse order they were opened. This last-opened-first-closed discipline is exactly what a stack models.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;
import java.util.Map;

public class ValidParentheses {
    public static boolean isValid(String s) {
        Deque<Character> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        Map<Character, Character> pairs = Map.of(')', '(', ']', '[', '}', '{');

        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
            if (pairs.containsValue(c)) {
                stack.push(c);
            } else if (pairs.containsKey(c)) {
                if (stack.isEmpty() || stack.pop() != pairs.get(c)) {
                    return false;
                }
            }
        }
        return stack.isEmpty();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(isValid("({[]})"));  // true
        System.out.println(isValid("({[)]"));   // false
        System.out.println(isValid("((("));     // false
    }
}
```

```text
true
false
false
```

### Why does this problem use a stack?

Brackets must close in the reverse order they opened. If you open round, then square, then curly, you must close curly first, then square, then round. This reverse ordering is exactly Last In First Out, which is the behavior a stack enforces. No other data structure naturally provides this guarantee.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class WhyStack {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Nested approvals: loan > document > signature
        Deque<String> approvals = new ArrayDeque<>();
        approvals.push("Loan approval opened");
        approvals.push("Document verification opened");
        approvals.push("Signature request opened");

        // Must close in reverse order
        System.out.println("Close: " + approvals.pop()); // Signature first
        System.out.println("Close: " + approvals.pop()); // Document second
        System.out.println("Close: " + approvals.pop()); // Loan last
    }
}
```

```text
Close: Signature request opened
Close: Document verification opened
Close: Loan approval opened
```

### How does the algorithm work step by step?

You scan the string left to right. For each opening bracket, push it onto the stack. For each closing bracket, check that the stack is not empty and that the popped top is the matching opener. If either check fails, return false immediately. After the full scan, return whether the stack is empty.

The matching logic uses a small map from each closer to its expected opener. For example, `)` maps to `(`, `]` maps to `[`, and `}` maps to `{`. On a closing bracket `c`, you look up `pairs.get(c)` and compare it to `stack.pop()`.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;
import java.util.Map;

public class StepByStepValidation {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String formula = "{[()]}";
        Deque<Character> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        Map<Character, Character> pairs = Map.of(')', '(', ']', '[', '}', '{');

        for (int i = 0; i < formula.length(); i++) {
            char c = formula.charAt(i);
            if (pairs.containsValue(c)) {
                stack.push(c);
                System.out.println("Index " + i + ": push '" + c + "' -> stack " + stack);
            } else {
                char top = stack.pop();
                boolean match = top == pairs.get(c);
                System.out.println("Index " + i + ": pop '" + top + "' vs expected '" + pairs.get(c) + "' -> " + (match ? "match" : "MISMATCH"));
            }
        }
        System.out.println("Valid: " + stack.isEmpty());
    }
}
```

```text
Index 0: push '{' -> stack [{]
Index 1: push '[' -> stack [[, {]
Index 2: push '(' -> stack [(, [, {]
Index 3: pop '(' vs expected '(' -> match
Index 4: pop '[' vs expected '[' -> match
Index 5: pop '{' vs expected '{' -> match
Valid: true
```

### What are the two failure conditions?

The first failure is a closing bracket arriving when the stack is empty. This means a closer has no matching opener, like a string that starts with `)`. The second failure is the string ending with brackets still on the stack, which means some openers were never closed. Both checks are essential for correctness.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;
import java.util.Map;

public class FailureCases {
    public static String validate(String s) {
        Deque<Character> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        Map<Character, Character> pairs = Map.of(')', '(', ']', '[', '}', '{');

        for (int i = 0; i < s.length(); i++) {
            char c = s.charAt(i);
            if (pairs.containsValue(c)) {
                stack.push(c);
            } else if (pairs.containsKey(c)) {
                if (stack.isEmpty()) return "Failure: unmatched closer at index " + i;
                if (stack.pop() != pairs.get(c)) return "Failure: wrong type at index " + i;
            }
        }
        return stack.isEmpty() ? "Valid" : "Failure: " + stack.size() + " unmatched openers";
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(validate(")("));    // unmatched closer
        System.out.println(validate("((("));   // unmatched openers
        System.out.println(validate("({)}")); // wrong type
        System.out.println(validate("()[]{}")); // valid
    }
}
```

```text
Failure: unmatched closer at index 0
Failure: 3 unmatched openers
Failure: wrong type at index 2
Valid
```

### What are the edge cases?

An empty string is valid because there are no unmatched brackets. A string of odd length can never be balanced because brackets pair in twos, so you can reject it in O(1). A single opener or a single closer is invalid. Nested and sequential combinations like `"({})[]"` must both pass.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class EdgeCases {
    public static boolean isValid(String s) {
        if (s.length() % 2 != 0) return false; // odd length shortcut
        java.util.Deque<Character> stack = new java.util.ArrayDeque<>();
        java.util.Map<Character, Character> pairs = java.util.Map.of(')', '(', ']', '[', '}', '{');
        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
            if (pairs.containsValue(c)) stack.push(c);
            else if (pairs.containsKey(c)) {
                if (stack.isEmpty() || stack.pop() != pairs.get(c)) return false;
            }
        }
        return stack.isEmpty();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Empty: " + isValid(""));         // true
        System.out.println("Odd: " + isValid("(()")); // false (odd shortcut)
        System.out.println("Single open: " + isValid("(")); // false
        System.out.println("Nested+seq: " + isValid("({})[]")); // true
    }
}
```

```text
Empty: true
Odd: false
Single open: false
Nested+seq: true
```

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is forgetting the final `stack.isEmpty()` check. Without it, a string of all openers like `"((("` passes because no mismatch occurs during the scan. Another mistake is using `==` to compare `Character` objects beyond the cached range; using `char` primitives avoids this.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class CommonMistakes {
    // WRONG: missing final isEmpty check
    public static boolean wrongValidation(String s) {
        Deque<Character> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
            if (c == '(' || c == '[' || c == '{') stack.push(c);
            else {
                if (stack.isEmpty()) return false;
                stack.pop(); // not even checking the type
            }
        }
        return true; // BUG: should be stack.isEmpty()
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Wrong accepts '(((':  " + wrongValidation("(((")); // true (wrong)
        System.out.println("Wrong accepts '({)}': " + wrongValidation("({)}")); // true (wrong)
    }
}
```

```text
Wrong accepts '(((':  true
Wrong accepts '({)}': true
```

### How is this applied in banking systems?

In the fee calculation engine at National Bank of India, fee formulas use nested brackets like `{(base + [tax * rate]) * discount}`. Before the formula parser evaluates the expression, a parentheses validator runs as a fast pre-check. If the brackets are unbalanced, the formula is rejected immediately and written to an audit log, protecting the parser from evaluating a corrupt or truncated expression.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;
import java.util.Map;

public class FeeFormulaValidator {
    private static final Map<Character, Character> PAIRS =
            Map.of(')', '(', ']', '[', '}', '{');

    public static boolean validateFormula(String formula) {
        Deque<Character> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        for (char c : formula.toCharArray()) {
            if (PAIRS.containsValue(c)) {
                stack.push(c);
            } else if (PAIRS.containsKey(c)) {
                if (stack.isEmpty() || stack.pop() != PAIRS.get(c)) {
                    return false;
                }
            }
            // skip non-bracket characters like digits and operators
        }
        return stack.isEmpty();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String good = "{(base + [tax * rate]) * discount}";
        String bad  = "{(base + [tax * rate) * discount}";

        System.out.println("Good formula valid: " + validateFormula(good));
        System.out.println("Bad formula valid:  " + validateFormula(bad));
    }
}
```

```text
Good formula valid: true
Bad formula valid:  false
```

### What about time and space complexity?

Time is O(n) because each character is visited once, and each character is pushed and popped at most once. Space is O(n) in the worst case, which happens when the entire string is openers. In practice, the stack grows only to the maximum nesting depth, which for well-formed formulas is much smaller than the string length.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class ComplexityAnalysis {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Worst case: all openers, stack grows to n
        String worst = "(((((((((("; // 10 openers
        System.out.println("Input length: " + worst.length());
        System.out.println("Stack max size: " + worst.length() + " (all openers)");

        // Best case: immediate mismatch, O(1) exit
        String best = ")";
        System.out.println("Immediate fail on: '" + best + "'");
    }
}
```

```text
Input length: 10
Stack max size: 10 (all openers)
Immediate fail on: ')'
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

This stack technique is the foundation of real parsing. The identical idea scales to matching HTML and XML tags, validating nested expressions, and checking balanced quotes. It is also the first step toward shunting-yard expression evaluation. Prefer `ArrayDeque` over the legacy `Stack` class because `Stack` extends `Vector` and synchronizes every call, which adds needless overhead in single-threaded parsing.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;
import java.util.Map;

public class ExtensibleValidator {
    // Easily extensible to new bracket types
    private static final Map<Character, Character> PAIRS = Map.of(
            ')', '(', ']', '[', '}', '{',
            '>', '<'  // custom angle brackets for XML-style tags
    );

    public static boolean isValid(String s) {
        Deque<Character> stack = new ArrayDeque<>();
        for (char c : s.toCharArray()) {
            if (PAIRS.containsValue(c)) stack.push(c);
            else if (PAIRS.containsKey(c)) {
                if (stack.isEmpty() || stack.pop() != PAIRS.get(c)) return false;
            }
        }
        return stack.isEmpty();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(isValid("<{[()]}>"));  // true with angle brackets
        System.out.println(isValid("<{[(>)]}"));  // false
    }
}
```

```text
true
false
```
